<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/encyclopedia/conflicts/20th-century">Conflicts &amp; Resolutions</a> · Crisis</p><h1 class="enc-title">Congo Crisis</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1960">1960</a>–<a href="/years/1965">1965</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Congo Crisis was the period of upheaval in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/republic-of-the-congo">Republic of the Congo</a>, now the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/democratic-republic-of-the-congo">Democratic Republic of the Congo</a>, from its independence from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/belgium">Belgium</a> in June 1960 to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mobutu-sese-seko">Mobutu Sese Seko</a>'s seizure of power in 1965. It began with an army mutiny, Belgian military intervention and the secession of the provinces of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/katanga">Katanga</a> and South <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kasai">Kasai</a>, and it drew in a large <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-nations">United Nations</a> force and the rival powers of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cold-war">Cold War</a>. Prime Minister <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/patrice-lumumba">Patrice Lumumba</a> was murdered in January 1961, and the UN Secretary-General <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dag-hammarskjold">Dag Hammarskjöld</a> died in a plane crash during the crisis that September.</p></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Background</h2><p>Colonial rule in the Congo began in the late 19th century with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/congo-free-state">Congo Free State</a>, the personal colony of King <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/leopold-ii-of-belgium">Leopold II of Belgium</a>, recognised internationally in 1885. Violence by its officials against Congolese and its system of economic extraction brought diplomatic pressure on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/belgium">Belgium</a>, which took control in 1908 and created the Belgian Congo. A nationalist movement grew among the évolués in the 1950s, divided into parties along ethnic and regional lines. The largest was the Mouvement National Congolais, in which <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/patrice-lumumba">Patrice Lumumba</a> became a leading figure.</p><p>At a Round Table Conference in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brussels">Brussels</a> in January 1960 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/belgium">Belgium</a>, which had hoped for at least thirty years before independence, was pressed into fixing 30 June 1960. Delegates did not agree on federalism, ethnicity or Belgium's future role. Few preparations had been made, and white technical experts stayed in post, which many Congolese resented.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Course of events</h2><p>In the first week of July 1960 the army mutinied and violence broke out between black and white civilians. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/belgium">Belgium</a> sent troops to protect fleeing whites. On 11 July Moïse Tshombe declared <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/katanga">Katanga</a> independent, saying 'We are seceding from chaos', and South Kasai also seceded. On 14 July the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-nations-security-council">United Nations Security Council</a> called for Belgian withdrawal and a UN force, but Secretary-General Dag Hammarskjöld would not use it against the secessionists. Lumumba then asked the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/soviet-union">Soviet Union</a> for help, which split the government. On 5 September President <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/joseph-kasavubu">Joseph Kasa-Vubu</a> dismissed him, and Colonel Joseph-Désiré Mobutu took control in a coup.</p><p>Lumumba was captured on 1 December 1960 and handed to Tshombe's forces, who executed him on 17 January 1961 with the help of Belgian officers. Hammarskjöld was killed in a plane crash in late 1961, and the UN then took a firmer line. With UN troops the central government ended the secessions of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/katanga">Katanga</a> and South Kasai by the start of 1963. In 1964 Maoist-inspired Simba rebels took territory in the east. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/belgium">Belgium</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a> intervened at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kisangani">Stanleyville</a> in November 1964, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mercenary">mercenaries</a> helped the army retake the ground. About 100,000 people are believed to have died.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Aftermath and significance</h2><p>After elections in March 1965 a new stalemate between Tshombe and Kasa-Vubu paralysed the government, and on 25 November 1965 Mobutu seized power in a bloodless coup. He centralised the state, created a single party in 1967 and renamed the country <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zaire">Zaire</a> in 1971. He remained in power until 1997. A Belgian inquiry led to an official apology in 2001 for moral responsibility, though not direct involvement, in Lumumba's killing. Historians usually portray the crisis as a time of intense disorder, and it was also a proxy conflict of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cold-war">Cold War</a>. Its turmoil helped to ignite the Portuguese Colonial War in neighbouring <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/angola">Angola</a>.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Congo Crisis” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
